    "result_text": "All 21 tests pass. \n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented address normalization applied whenever an owner is created, in `OwnerRestControllerV1.java`.\n\n**Changes made:**\n\n1. **New `normalizeAddress` helper** \u2014 trims and collapses whitespace, upper-cases, and expands whole-word abbreviations (`ST\u2192STREET`, `RD\u2192ROAD`, `AVE\u2192AVENUE`) via a small `expandAddressAbbreviation` switch. Returns `\"\"` for null/blank input so callers can treat it as blank.\n\n2. **`addOwner` now stores the normalized address** \u2014 `owner.setAddress(normalizeAddress(owner.getAddress()))` is set right after mapping, before the household/duplicate logic runs. Since the stored value is normalized and the existing household-duplicate detection and shared-household-id derivation already compare `owner.getAddress()` (both sides passed through `normalizeForComparison`), all address comparisons now operate on the normalized form.\n\n3. **Required-field check uses the normalized form** \u2014 `rejectMissingOrBlankFields` checks `isBlank(normalizeAddress(fields.getAddress()))`, so an address blank after normalization is rejected.\n\nThis satisfies the new behaviour: normalized addresses are stored and returned (e.g. `\"  12  main  st \"` \u2192 `\"12 MAIN STREET\"`, `\"7 elm ave\"` \u2192 `\"7 ELM AVENUE\"`), a whitespace-only address is rejected, and normalized-equal addresses collide for both duplicate detection (Cp10) and shared household ids (Cp11). The full suite (21 tests) is green.",
